  {
    "episode_id": 90477,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90477.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 241,
    "turn_text": "how the economy works, and started filling up with all kinds of conspiracy theorizing that became kind of common or conventional wisdom within the Republican Party base. That hasn't happened in the Democratic Party. I think people like the passion that Bernie brought, but Bernie Sanders is a pretty centrist politician relative to Corbin or relative to some of the Republicans. And so I don't worry about that. What I do worry about is that in an era where we are looking for simple solutions and want 100% of what we want and when we want it. that we end up starting to shut ourselves off from different points of view, shutting down debate, becoming more dogmatic, becoming more brittle. And I don't see that being a successful strategy for us winning over the country. Remember, we won the popular vote. You know, we don't have very good population distribution from a",
